Cell["\<\
Check: {0.000699, True}. In Wolfram: NetTrain[\[Ellipsis], Method -> \
{\"ADAM\", \"Beta1\" -> 0.9, \"Beta2\" -> 0.98, \"LearningRate\" -> peak, \
\"LearningRateSchedule\" -> (warmRate[#]/peak &)}]; the schedule is a factor \
of the learning rate. Unit 4 does this.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a64286af-11e9-437e-bf37-c3e8daf6f5db"],

Cell["13. Beam search with a length penalty", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"284f15f2-f6e2-4136-8678-97a74c12cf01"],

Cell["\<\
Unit 1\[CloseCurlyQuote]s beam search keeps the B best partial outputs. Its \
scores are sums of log-probabilities, which only fall as a hypothesis grows, \
so plain beam search prefers short outputs. The paper uses beam size 4 and \
divides each finished hypothesis\[CloseCurlyQuote]s log-probability by a \
length penalty with \[Alpha] = 0.6 (Section 6.1, from Wu et al. 2016):\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2db0ea06-8cbb-433f-954d-329348313a50"],

Cell["\<\
score(Y) = log P(Y | X) / lp(Y),        lp(Y) = ((5 + |Y|) / 6)^\[Alpha]\
\>", "Text",
 TextAlignment->Center,
 FontFamily->"Times",
 FontSize->15,
 FontSlant->"Italic",ExpressionUUID->"a964ef0f-fc14-4f48-9e0d-e2ec8aaa6dcc"],

Cell["\<\
A short hypothesis with log P = \[Minus]2.0 and 2 tokens, a long one with \
\[Minus]2.6 and 8 tokens. Without the penalty the short one wins (\[Minus]2.0 \
> \[Minus]2.6). With \[Alpha] = 0.6: lp(2) = (7/6)^0.6 = 1.097 and lp(8) = \
(13/6)^0.6 = 1.590, so the scores are \[Minus]2.0/1.097 = \[Minus]1.823 and \
\[Minus]2.6/1.590 = \[Minus]1.635. Now the long one wins.\
\>", "Text",
 CellFrame->{{3, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.83, 0.34, 0.11],
 Background->RGBColor[
  1, 0.97, 0.92],ExpressionUUID->"c916acbe-de07-4f5d-9e53-c8d950bcc160"],

