Cell["\<\
1: (0.804, 0.196): a better match gives more weight to v\:2081. 2: 4. 3: (sin \
2, cos 2, sin 0.02, cos 0.02) = (0.909, \[Minus]0.416, 0.020, 0.9998). 4: \
0.91. 5: step 16 000, since the rate falls as 1/\[Sqrt]step.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5ca2a760-bdb2-416c-b018-e4951225e9e2"],

Cell["16. Summary", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"f9af53fe-39eb-436e-8e5a-1aaaf70c2a59"],

Cell["\<\
Attention is a weighted average: softmax(QK\:1d40/\[Sqrt]d\:2096)V, where the \
\[Sqrt]d\:2096 keeps the softmax from saturating. Masks decide who may look \
at whom. Several heads attend in different subspaces for the price of one. \
Sinusoidal codes add order in a way that makes shifts linear. Each sub-layer \
is wrapped as LayerNorm(x + Sublayer(x)), and an encoder and a decoder layer \
are built from these parts alone. Shared embeddings, label smoothing, warm-up \
and a length penalty complete the recipe. Every part built from primitive \
Wolfram layers equals its array version.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6a1c8398-bb19-4986-96e6-abd647a4e66a"],

Cell["\<\
Next: Unit 4 assembles these nets into a transformer that expands \:2a02/\
\:2a01 expressions, and trains it.\
